es positive gap. With the same Monge endpoint coupling and endpoint cost, a Brownian bridge has positive gap, and projecting the same straight bridge to a lower-dimensional visible state also creates positive gap. Thus the gap is not determined solely by endpoint transport cost or velocity energy; it depends on the triple $(\\mathscr Q,U,\\mathcal O)$.\n'''\nwith open(OUT_ROOT/'rebuttal_exact3d_paragraph.tex','w') as f: f.write(paragraph)\nprint('\\nSaved outputs to', OUT_ROOT)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"id":"93bca502","cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n## Interpretation for rebuttal\n\nUse the following distinction.\n\nThe raw proxy gap is an **absolute regression burden**. It is expected to track flow-matching training loss because the loss is an absolute squared-error objective.\n\nThe normalized ratio \\(\\eta\\) is a **relative unexplained-variance fraction**. It asks whether the Markov state explains a larger fraction of the velocity variance. This ratio may move differently from the raw gap. If OT reduces raw gap but increases or does not improve \\(\\eta\\), the correct interpretation is that OT reduces the absolute burden largely through shorter/lower-energy endpoint transport. That is still useful for training, but it is not evidence of transport-cost-free conditional ambiguity reduction.\n\nTherefore, for rebuttal, lead with the exact 3D Gaussian--OT control when answering whether the theoretical Markovization gap is more than transport cost. Use CIFAR-100/CIFAR-10/Fashion-MNIST proxies only as practical within-dataset ranking diagnostics.\n","metadata":{}}]}